  "passage": {
    "title": "Learning a Language as an Adult",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Many people believe that children learn languages more easily than adults, and that after a certain age it becomes almost impossible to speak a new language well. This idea is often called the critical period theory. But is it true?"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Children do have some advantages. They pick up pronunciation quickly, and young children who move abroad often speak like local people within a year or two. They are also less afraid of making mistakes. However, research suggests that the advantage is smaller than most people think. Adults and teenagers actually learn faster at the beginning, because they can use grammar rules and study methods."
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Adults face different challenges. They are usually busier, with work and family taking up much of their time. They may also worry about sounding silly, which makes them speak less and practise less. Yet adults have strengths that children lack: they know how to study, they understand why they are learning, and they can compare the new language with their own."
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "What is the best way for adults to learn? Experts give a few practical suggestions. First, study little and often. Twenty minutes every day is more effective than three hours once a week. Second, speak from the beginning, even if you make mistakes. Third, use the language for something you enjoy, such as films, music or sport, so that learning does not feel like work. Finally, be patient. It usually takes between six hundred and a thousand hours to reach a comfortable level in a language like Spanish or French."
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Learning a language also keeps the brain active. Studies suggest that people who speak two or more languages may develop serious memory problems later than those who speak only one. So whatever your age, it is never too late to start."
      }
    ]
  },
